@*
    Renders NavGroup.Icon / NavItem.Icon.

    The model types that property as "icon identifier OR inline SVG markup", and in practice three
    different shapes arrive here. Injecting all of them as raw markup — which is what this used to
    do — only works for one:

      1. A full <svg …>…</svg> element. Hand-written icons look like this. Passes through.

      2. An inner SVG FRAGMENT: <g>, <path>, <rect>. This is what every MudBlazor constant is —
         Icons.Material.Filled.Handshake is 748 characters starting "<g><rect fill=…". MudIcon
         wraps it in its own <svg> before rendering; we did not, and an SVG element outside an
         <svg> root draws nothing. Every icon in the sidebar was silently invisible. Wrapped here
         in the 24x24 viewBox those constants are authored against.

      3. A bare NAME — Icon = "Newspaper". Not markup at all, so it landed in the DOM as literal
         TEXT inside an 18x18 flex:0 0 auto box with overflow visible. "Newspaper" needs 45px,
         gets 18, and the other 27 paint straight over the label next to it. That is what turned
         two nav groups into "EWSPAPERCONTENT" stacked on their own children. There is no way to
         resolve a name to its glyph without reflecting over the ~2000 constants in
         Icons.Material.Filled, which would take the trimmer's guarantees with it — so a name is
         dropped rather than rendered. Pass Icons.Material.Filled.Newspaper, not "Newspaper".

    .znav-icon also clips now, so a fourth shape nobody predicted degrades to an empty box instead
    of corrupting its neighbours.
*@
@namespace Zonit.Dashboard.Components.Layouts

@if (_kind is Kind.Document)
{
    <span class="znav-icon" aria-hidden="true">@((MarkupString)Icon!)</span>
}
else if (_kind is Kind.Fragment)
{
    <span class="znav-icon" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" focusable="false">@((MarkupString)Icon!)</svg>
    </span>
}
else if (Reserve)
{
    @* Empty slot, same width. A list where only some entries declare an icon otherwise renders
       with its labels at two different left edges, and the eye reads that as broken alignment
       rather than as "this one has no icon". The parent decides: the slot is reserved only when
       at least one sibling in the same list actually has one, so a wholly icon-less nav keeps
       its labels flush left instead of carrying a column of nothing. *@
    <span class="znav-icon znav-icon-empty" aria-hidden="true"></span>
}

@code {
    [Parameter]
    public string? Icon { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Render an empty slot when there is no icon, to keep a mixed list aligned.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Reserve { get; set; }

    private enum Kind { None, Document, Fragment }

    private Kind _kind;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        var value = Icon?.TrimStart();

        _kind = value switch
        {
            null or "" => Kind.None,
            _ when value.StartsWith("<svg", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase) => Kind.Document,
            _ when value[0] == '<' => Kind.Fragment,
            _ => Kind.None,
        };
    }
}
